    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d171">

                <pb facs="0309" n="213" sameAs="frus:frus1872p1/pg213"/>

                <head>No. 171.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Fish
                        to Mr. Robeson.</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1872-04-06"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 6,
                        1872</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Since the letter to you from this
                    Department, of the 29th ultimo, relative to the abrogation of the treaty between
                    Great Britain and the Sultan of Zanzibar or Muscat, this Department has been
                    orally informed by Sir Edward Thornton, the British minister here, that it is
                    not the wish of his government to have the whole of that instrument annulled,
                    but only that provision of the treaty by which the right to transport domestic
                    slaves, by sea, within certain limits of their own territory, was reserved. I
                    will consequently thank you to modify accordingly any orders which have been or
                    may be given by the Navy Department upon the subject.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>HAMILTON FISH.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
